It's off the intended topic, but...

Rakshasa, in their descriptions in the D&D Monster Manual, always have
their palms where the back of their hands should be, and vice-versa,
no matter what form they take.  They're shapeshifters, and can make
themselves appear to be any humanoid they like... but no matter what,
their hands are always wrong.  It's the only way to tell them from
real people besides True Seeing (a 5th-level spell).

Of course, most rakshasa a DM would use in-game would know to keep
their hands in their pockets, in the sleeves of big billowy robes, or
train themselves to use their hands backwards.

